# Covalent Organic Frameworks Based Heterostructure in Solar‐To‐Fuel Conversion

## abstract
Photocatalytic reactions for fuel generation are crucial for the world's energy needs. Covalent‐Organic‐Frameworks (COFs) have been extensively studied as promising designable photocatalysts for these reactions due to their efficient visible‐light absorption, suitable energy‐band structure, facilitated intramolecular charge separation, and fast mass transfer. However, the activities of pristine COFs remain unsatisfactory, due to intermolecular charge recombination. Recently, COF‐based heterostructures, which combine COFs with metal‐sulfides, metal‐oxides, carbon materials, or MOFs, have attracted increasing attention for enhancing solar‐to‐fuel conversion efficiency by facilitating interfacial photo‐generated carrier separation, sensitizing wide‐gap semiconductors, and promoting surface redox reactions. Thus, a review of the state‐of‐the‐art progress of COF‐based heterostructure photocatalysts in reactions such as H2 evolution, CO2 reduction, O2 evolution, H2O splitting and CO2 splitting is crucial for the design of new photocatalysts to promote solar‐to‐fuel conversion. In this review, the COF‐based heterostructures photocatalysts are highlighted based on their synthesis, properties, and reasons for enhanced activities. Moreover, design principles are raised for such photocatalysts for each fuel generation reaction, based on insights into related research. Finally, this review is concluded by proposing future trends for COF‐based heterostructures photocatalysts, with attention to the design of COFs and supports, analyzing the photocatalytic reaction dynamics, together with considering practical applications.

This review summarizes the composition, synthetic methods, and the prepared reactions for different types of COFbased Heterostructures, proposes the reasons for the enhanced photocatalytic activities, and raises the design principle for effective COF-based Heterostructure for target photocatalytic reactions. The trend for future development of COFbased Heterostructure photocatalysts is proposed.
Photocatalytic reactions for fuel generation are crucial for the world’s energy needs. Covalent-Organic-Frameworks (COFs) have been extensively studied as promising designable photocatalysts for these reactions due to their efficient visible-light absorption, suitable energy-band structure, facilitated intramolecular charge separation, and fast mass transfer. However, the activities of pristine COFs remain unsatisfactory, due to intermolecular charge recombination. Recently, COF-based heterostructures, which combine COFs with metal-sulfides, metal-oxides, carbon materials, or MOFs, have attracted increasing attention for enhancing solar-to-fuel conversion efficiency by facilitating interfacial photo-generated carrier separation, sensitizing wide-gap semiconductors, and promoting surface redox reactions. Thus, a review of the state-of-the-art progress of COF-based heterostructure photocatalysts in reactions such as $\mathsf{H}_{2}$ evolution, $\mathsf{c o}_{2}$ reduction, $\b{\circ}_{2}$ evolution, $H_{2}O$ splitting and $\mathsf{c o}_{2}$ splitting is crucial for the design of new photocatalysts to promote solar-to-fuel conversion. In this review, the COF-based heterostructures photocatalysts are highlighted based on their synthesis, properties, and reasons for enhanced activities. Moreover, design principles are raised for such photocatalysts for each fuel generation reaction, based on insights into related research. Finally, this review is concluded by proposing future trends for COF-based heterostructures photocatalysts, with attention to the design of COFs and supports, analyzing the photocatalytic reaction dynamics, together with considering practical applications.
## 1. Introduction
Global industrialization and urbanization inevitably lead to an energy and environmental crisis. For the sustainable development of mankind, the replacement of traditional fossil fuels with renewable ones is a promising strategy in the face of such a crisis, and thus has become the focus of scientific research.[1–4] The green and environmentally friendly semiconductor photocatalysis has well addressed the above concerns, as it utilizes the inexhaustible solar irradiation to obtain the energy sources in the form of hydrogen $\left(\operatorname{H}_{2}\right)$ and carbon monoxide (CO) in an almost pollutant-free manner. Consequently, it has gained increasing attention after the first successful realization of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ generation in 1972 by Fujishima & Honda et al. on the $\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ electrode under irradiation.[5]
As the key component in the photocatalytic system, many kinds of semiconductor photocatalysts, such as $\mathrm{TiO}_{2},\sp[\lbrack6-9\rbrack\ \mathrm{WO}_{3}$ ,[10] ${\mathrm{Bi}}_{2}{\tt W O}_{6}$ ,[11,12] CdS,[13,14] as well as carbon nitride,[15,16] and others, have been well developed. However, it is difficult for these photocatalysts to simultaneously fulfill the following requirements for an ideal photocatalyst: 1) highly responsive to solar irradiation; 2) adequate redox potential for the reaction and abundant redox active sites for the reaction; 3) fast mass transfer and adsorption of the reactants; 4) environmentally friendly and high crustal abundance.[17] Fortunately, in 2005, the discovery of Covalent Organic Frameworks (COFs) by Yaghi et al. gave researchers hope for effective photocatalysts.[18] COFs are composed of a variety of organic molecular building blocks that are periodically ordered via covalent bonds. These bonds include imine, triazine, $\beta$ -ketoenamine, hydrazone, olefin, azine, and others.[19] These structural features grant COF with many advantages, such as suitable energy band structure, designability of periodically ordered structures, high surface area, surface accessibility for reactants, and low density. As a result, research on solar-to-fuel conversion using COFs as photocatalysts has increased steadily over the decades.
As with other semiconductor photocatalysts, the insufficient separation of the electron–hole pair generated under irradiation is considered to be one of the major bottlenecks for raising the efficiency of COFs photocatalysts.[20,21] Currently, the predominant method to improve the charge separation in photocatalysis is to construct the heterostructure between semiconductors. Heterostructure is defined as the composition of two or more types of semiconductors. According to the relative positions of the energy band of the composing semiconductors, the heterostructure can be classified into three types. The heterostructure composed of semiconductors with a straddling energy band structure is defined “Type I” heterostructure, the heterostructure composed of semiconductors with a staggered energy band structure is defined “Type II” heterostructure, while the heterostructure composed of semiconductors with a broken energy band structure is defined “Type III” heterostructure. Among them, the Type II heterostructures, which are composed of semiconductors with staggered energy band structure, are particularly attractive because this type of heterostructure is effective for charge separation.[22] However, the Type II heterostructure promotes charge separation at the cost of compromising the redox capability for the photogenerated carriers. Fortunately, in the 2010s, a novel concept called all solid states direct Z-Scheme heterostructure has been proposed.[23–25] The Z-Scheme heterostructure is composed of semiconductors with an energy band structure similar to that of Type II, but the photo-generated carriers are separated in a different way. As a result, the photo-generated carriers with higher redox potentials are retained for corresponding reactions. Recently, a newly concept of Step Scheme (S-Scheme) heterostructure has been proposed, based on a deeper consideration of the banding of the energy band structure in the photo-generated charge separation processes. More detailed discussions on the difference between the Type II heterostructure and the Z-Scheme heterostructure can be found in other remarkable papers.[26–28] On the other hand, for many types of large bandgap photocatalysts, such as the most studied anatase $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ , the limited absorption of visible light irradiation is another barrier to enhance their photocatalytic activity. In this aspect, the Type II heterostructures between the COF with smaller band gap and metal oxides can, first of all, promote the separation of the carriers, moreover extending the absorption range of the heterostructures to visible light.[22,29,30] Moreover, for most semiconductors, the mass transfer of reactants to the active sites is another barrier in the photocatalytic reaction as a result of the non-porous surface texture. In this respect, the COFs with nano-sized porous frameworks are effective in facilitating the mass transfer by templating the growth of the semiconductor.[31] In addition, the COFs act as a protective layer for semiconductors such as CdS, inhibiting its photo-corrosion. At the same time, the stability of the photocatalysts is improved and the adverse effects on the environment are eliminated.[32,33]
Based on the above discussions, it can be expected that COFbased heterostructures are promising photocatalysts for fuel generation reaction.[19,34] The first report on COF-based heterostructure for photocatalytic fuel generation reaction was published in 2014.[35] Since then, there has been a surge of research in this field. The COFs include TpPA,[35–39] LZU,[40] CTF,[41] TD,[42] TpBD,[43] and $318^{[17,44]}$ et al. And the combined materials include $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ,[17,45–47] CdS,[35,48–53] $\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ ,[43,54–58] and metal–organic frameworks[37,39,41,59–63] et al. (For ease of discussion, the sum of these materials is sometimes been referred to as the support in this manuscript). These heterostructures show outstanding performances in photocatalytic fuel production processes, e.g., hydrogen evolution reaction (HER), carbon dioxide reduction reaction (CDRR), $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ splitting together with $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ splitting. Therefore, a review that systematically summarizes the current progress in COF-based heterostructure photocatalysts for fuel production reactions and raises the principles for the design of effective COF-based heterostructure solar-to-fuel photocatalysts is significant. Herein, we have reviewed and discussed the progress of COF-based heterostructures in photocatalytic fuel production based on the preparation methods, the functions of the compositions, and the possible reaction mechanisms. The design principle for COF-based heterostructures targeting different photocatalytic fuel production reactions were also raised. An early access to the future trend along with possible challenges on COF-based heterostructure photocatalytic fuel production reaction based on the limits of current progress was given at the end of the manuscript. It is hoped that this review could deepen the understanding from design, synthesis to application of innovative and effective COF-based heterostructures in photocatalytic fuel production. It is also expected to provide constructive guidance for future studies at the end of the manuscript. A brief summary of the types, synthesis, applications and preferred reactions for COF-based heterostructures is given in Figure 1.
## 2. The Types, Preparation Strategies, Characteristics, and Applied Reactions for COF-Based Heterostructure
Based on the chemical composition of the support, heterostructures are divided into five types in this review: 1) COF/metal sulfide (abbreviated as COF/MS), 2) COF/metal oxide (abbreviated as COF/MO), 3) COF/carbon materials (abbreviated as COF/Car.), 4) COF/metal–organic frameworks (abbreviated as COF/MOF), and 5) COF/other (metal phosphide & clusters). The composites of COF and pure metals in various forms are not discussed in this review.
The preparation strategies for a particular type of COF-based heterostructure depends on the character of the composition. These strategies can be defined as: 1) the in situ synthesis of COF: the synthesis of COF from the molecular building units on the surface of the as-prepared support; 2) the in situ synthesis of the support: the synthesis of the support from the precursor on the
COF; and 3) the combination of the as-prepared components. By summarizing the results of related research, it can be observed that the supports are always confined in the pores of the COF in its in situ synthesis on COF. However, this type of support distribution is rarely found in the heterostructures prepared by in situ synthesis of COF and the combination of the as-prepared components. The schematic illustration of these strategies is depicted in Figure 2. For the COF/metal sulfide heterostructure, the preparation methods include both the in situ synthesis of the COF from the molecular monomers on the as-prepared metal sulfide[50–54,64–66] and the in situ synthesis of the metal sulfide on the as-prepared COF. For the former method, solvothermal treatments of the COF monomers and the as-prepared metal sulfide nanoparticles (NPs) were carried out under conditions similar to those used for the synthesis of COFs.[35,48,49] It should be mentioned that in order to strengthen the covalent bond between the most commonly studied imine-linked COFs and the supports (mainly metal sulfides and oxides), 3-aminopropyltriethoxysilane (APTES) is often employed to modify the support surface prior to the in situ growth of COFs. For the in situ synthesis of metal sulfides on the surfaces of the COFs, the solvothermal treatment of the metal precursor and the sulfide precursor (sometimes the sulfide precursor can be the solvent containing the S element) is commonly used. In particular, it has recently been discovered that by exploiting the photocatalytic characteristics of the as-synthesized COFs, the metal sulfides can be assembled in situ on the COF surface via a photo-deposition strategy without additional heating processes. In the photo-deposition processes, the electrons in the conduction band (C.B.) of the COFs contribute to the reductive formation of $\mathsf{S}^{2-}$ . The metal sulfide NPs with uniform size distribution can be obtained by photo-deposition. In addition, the photo-deposition strategy leads to a more intimate contact at the interface. For the COF/metal oxide heterostructure, in most cases the COFs were constructed in situ on the asprepared metal oxide surface. This may be because, first, the majority of metal oxide photocatalysts were synthesized using the water-sensitive salts or organics as precursors. The trace amount of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ in the pores of the COFs or during the synthesis of the COFs may lead to the uncontrolled hydrolysis of the precursor, which is detrimental to the texture of the prepared metal oxide nanocrystal. In addition, the crystallization of many metal oxides requires thermal treatments in an oxidizing atmosphere. Undoubtedly, these processes would lead to the decomposition of the COF. In order to avoid the above circumstances, the formation of the stable crystalline is a prerequisite before its combination with the COF to form the heterostructure. Recently, the supercritical (SC) $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ route facilitate the diffusion of the titanium precursor into the pores of the as-prepared COF which contributes to the formation of ${\mathrm{COF}}/{\mathrm{TiO}}_{2}$ heterostructure with COF spheres as the shell after solvothermal treatment.[46] The preparation methods for the COF/carbon heterostructure vary with the diversity of carbon materials. For the $\mathrm{COF}/\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ and COF/TMX heterostructures, with few exceptions, the COF are assembled in situ on the surface of the as-synthesized supports from the condensation of the monomers or the crystallization transformation of the amorphous polymer. This is because the supports are prepared by heat treatments. For the $\mathrm{COF/rGO}$ heterostructures, the treatment of the COF monomers with the GO may not only contribute to the in situ crystallization of the COF, but also to the reduction of the GO to rGO with strengthened bonding at the interfaces. In addition, the reduction of GO can also be carried out on the as-synthesized COF surface by a photo-deposition strategy. For the COF/MOF heterostructures, both the in situ synthesis of COFs and the in situ synthesis of MOFs from their corresponding monomers are predominant, as the reactions for the formation of the frameworks can be carried out within the pores of the as-synthesized frameworks. Moreover, the heterostructure can be constructed by combining both the as-synthesized COFs and MOFs via solvothermal treatment or simple mechanical lattice forming, as both the as-synthesized COFs and MOFs are rich in surface functionalized groups for covalent bonding. Based on the above discussions, it can be expected that in the future, besides the above-mentioned methods, the simultaneous self-assembly of COF and support from precursors may be carried out and focused on, as this method may facilitate the formation of more intimate heterostructure interfaces and better configuration.
The characteristics of the COF-based heterostructure are mutually influenced by the COF and the supports. For the COF/MS, the heterostructures show effective absorption of visible light irradiation and appropriate energy band structure for photocatalytic fuel production reactions. In addition, some MS are Ptfree cocatalysts for photocatalytic HER. However, the problems for the MSs are the difficulties in the synthesis of the monolayer nanosheet and the photo-corrosion under irradiation. For the COF/MOs, the heterostructures are stable under irradiation and usually environmentally friendly. In addition, the MOs promote the adsorption and activation of the reactants. However, the MOs usually have large band gaps and lower C.B. potentials. This is not desirable for the reduction reactions when the Type II heterostructures are constructed. For the COF/Car., the heterostructures are rich in structure, low cost and environmentally friendly. The drawbacks are the harsh preparation process (Hummer’s method for graphene oxides) and the dependence of expensive Pt cocatalyst for $\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ in HER. For the COF/MOF, the heterostructures have high specific surface areas, uniform pores and rich chemical structure. However, in order to promote the covalent bonding between the MOFs and the COFs, $\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ functional groups are always required for the ligand of the MOFs, as the COFs are mainly constructed via imine bonds.
Based on their characteristics discussed above, different types of COF-based heterostructures are applied in various photocatalytic reactions. For the COF/MS heterostructures, the researches in HER and CDRR are conducted. While for the COF/MO heterostructures, HER, CDRR, $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ splitting and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ splitting reactions are well studied. For the COF/MOF, the reactions currently applied include HER and CDRR. For COF/MOF, HER and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ splitting are focused. In addition, HER and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ splitting are the preferred reactions for the COF/cluster and COF/metal phosphide heterostructures.
## 3. COF-Based Heterostructure in Photocatalytic Fuel Generation
## 3.1. Hydrogen Evolution Reaction (HER)
Photocatalytic HER offers an environmentally friendly strategy for the production of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ . The highest calorific value and nonpolluting properties make $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ the most promising candidate to bridge the energy gap in the future. The first study on photocatalytic HER via COF-based heterostructure was carried out by Banerjee et al. in 2014. This is also the first and most studied fuel generation reaction for COF-based heterostructure photocatalysts. So far, the heterostructure consists of COFs such as TPPA,
TTA, TPBD, EB, LZU et al. and the supports such as metal sulfide, metal oxide, carbon nitride, graphene derivatives, MOFs as well as metal phosphide. The highest HER rate for the COF-based heterostructure is $47.6\ \mathrm{mmol}$ $\mathbf{g}^{-1}\mathbf{h}^{-1}$ under visible light irradiation. In the following section, COF-based heterostructure HER photocatalysts are discussed based on their development timeline and types.
## 3.1.1. Photocatalytic HER Based on COF/MSs Heterostructure
Based on the functions of the metal sulfides in the heterostructure photocatalysts for HER, the COF/MS heterostructures are divided into two categories. The first type is $\mathrm{COF/CdS\&COF/In}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{3}$ heterostructures, where the sulfides in the heterostructure also act as photocatalysts. Another type is ${\mathrm{COF/MS}}_{\mathrm{x}}$ $\mathrm{~\textmu~}\mathrm{~M}=\mathrm{M}\mathrm{{o}}$ , Ni & Sn) heterostructures, where the $\mathrm{MS}_{\mathrm{x}}$ act as cocatalysts in the heterostructure.
The first report on COF-based heterostructure for photocatalytic HER is TpPA-2/CdS, which was carried out by Banerjee et al. in 2014.[35] The TpPA-2 COF was pre-synthesized and selected as the matrix to support the CdS NPs via solvothermal methods (Figure 3a,b). As a result of its high stability and porosity, proper energy band position and $\pi$ -conjugated 2D crystalline framework, the COF facilitated the separation of the photoinduced carriers in the CdS NPs via the Type II heterostructure. As a result, the optimal TpPA-2/CdS heterostructure with a mass ratio of 1/9 exhibits ${\approx}29\$ -fold enhanced photocatalytic HER activity to $3678\ \mathrm{\textmumol\h^{-1}\ g^{-1}}$ under visible light irradiation (Figure 3c). Meanwhile, the stability for CdS NPs was promoted, resulting from their immobilization on the stable framework of TpPA-2. This pioneering work demonstrates the remarkable potential of COF-based heterostructure in constructing an effective photocatalytic HER system under visible light irradiation.
Similar work has been carried out by Zou & Luo et al. in 2018 and 2020.[48,49] In their works, the CTF COF serves as both support and electron donor photocatalysts in the Type II heterostructure (Figure 4a). It is interesting to note that in their works, the novel photo deposition method was more effective in the preparation of CdS on the surface of the CTF COF (CdS-CTF-1). Compared to the heterostructures prepared by traditional solvothermal methods (Figure 4b,c), the CdS-CTF-1 shows a further enhanced photocatalytic HER activity to $11.43\mathrm{mmol}\mathrm{h}^{-1}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ under visible light irradiation (Figure 4d,e). The improvements are not only due to more exposed active sites in CdS NPs with smaller size, but also to a more efficient electron injection between the components with more intimate contacts. In addition, the photodeposition process is carried out at a lower temperature without the use of toxic agents and complex procedures. Therefore, it is expected that the photo-deposition method can be a promising method for larger scale heterostructure fabrication.
A more effective configuration for the recently proposed COF/CdS heterostructure is the assembly of the corresponding nanosheets to form the 2D–2D heterostructure. In the work of Li et al. in 2023,[50] heterostructures with this 2D–2D configuration are constructed by in situ synthesis of TpTAP COF from corresponding molecular building blocks on the surface of the as-prepared CdS nanosheet (Figure 5a–c). As expected, the heterostructures exhibit intriguing properties such as outstanding surface texture, intimate interfaces and a large number of surface reactive sites. Under visible light irradiation (lambda $>$ $420\mathrm{nm})$ ), the heterostructure exhibits a remarkable HER activity of $47.6\mathrm{mmol}\mathrm{h}^{-1}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . Based on these results, it can be concluded that COF/CdS heterostructures are effective photocatalysts for HER.
Besides, the $\mathbf{CdS@COF}$ heterostructure with core/shell configuration is also significant. Heterostructure with such configuration may facilitates the mass transfer of the reactants to the active sites, and the first related work was processed by Jiang’s group in 2021.[51] In their research, the heterostructures were constructed by in situ growth of TpPA COF from the corresponding monomers on the surface of the CdS. By optimizing the thickness of the COF shell, an enhanced photocatalytic HER activity of $24.3~\mathrm{mmol~h^{-1}~g^{-1}}$ was realized under irradiation with wavelength $>420\mathrm{nm}$ .
However, the main problem for CdS-based photocatalysts is photo-corrosion, which not only reduces the photocatalytic activity, but also the highly toxic $\mathrm{Cd^{2+}}$ can cause environmental problems. Two strategies have been proposed to address this problem, one is to design and prepare the heterostructure with COFs to protect the CdS. The other is to search for efficient metal sulfide photocatalysts that are environmentally friendly.
For the first strategy, in the work of Liu et al. in 2021,[52] the T-COF was assembled in situ from its precursors M1 (1,3,5- benzenetricarboxaldehyde) and M2 (2,4,6-tris(4-aminophenyl)- 1,3,5-triazine) on the surface of cadmium sulfide nanoparticles to form the core/shell configuration (Figure 5d). The photo-corrosion of the CdS was inhibited by the protective effect of the tightly binding T-COF. Furthermore, the constructed Z-Scheme heterostructure prevents the recombination of photo-induced electron–hole pairs, resulting in an Apparent Quantum Efficiency (AQE) of $37.8\%$ under monochromatic light irradiation at $\lambda=365~\mathrm{{nm}}$ . The optimized CdS/COF heterostructure shows a photocatalytic HER activity of up to 12.25 mmol $\ensuremath{\mathrm{h^{-1}~}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{g^{-1}}}$ . Later, a similar work was carried out in 2022, the TTI-COF was assembled from its molecular monomer units, on the CdS surface modified with $\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ , as it is discovered by Cao’s group lately,[53] the $\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ modified cadmium sulfide nanorods (CdS NRs) were first solvothermally synthesized in ethylenediamine (Figure 5e,f). The TTI-COF-60 was then constructed by covalently bonding the monomers on the surface of the $\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ CdS nanorods. This constructed core/shell heterostructure not only enhanced the photocatalytic activities by promoting charge separation, but also stabilized the CdS via the $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C}}={\mathrm{N}}$ covalent bonds between the COF shell and the CdS core. The heterostructure shows a remarkable enhanced photocatalytic HER activity up to 2.8517 mmol $\ensuremath{\mathrm{h^{-1}~}}\ensuremath{\mathbf{g}}^{-1}$ , under the irradiation of visible light, in the absence of Pt cocatalyst.
For the second strategy, the ${\mathrm{COF}}/{\mathrm{In}}_{2}{\mathrm{S}}_{3}$ heterostructure was investigated.[64] This is because $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{3}$ is an environmentally friendly photocatalyst compared to the highly toxic Cd element, without compromising the activity. In the work of Cheng et al, a novel hyper-crosslinked $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{3}@\mathrm{HLZU}-1$ was obtained by a molecular expansion strategy. The LZU-1 COF was synthesized and hyper-crosslinked by 1,2-dichloroethane stacking. The indium element was first introduced into the pores of the stacked HLZU-1 in the form of MIL-68(In), and then the $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{3}$ was prepared by vulcanization with thiourea. It is found that the hypercrosslinked stacking configuration of the heterostructure is crucial for the remarkably enhanced photocatalytic HER activity up to 11.92 mmol $\mathbf{g}^{-1}\mathbf{h}^{-1}$ under irradiation $\lambda>420\ \mathrm{nm}$ , since through this configuration an intimate interface was constructed to facilitate charge separation via the Z-Scheme. The brief summary of the photocatalytic performances of the COF/CdS and ${\mathrm{COF}}/{\mathrm{In}}_{2}{\mathrm{S}}_{3}$ heterostructures in HER are listed in Table 1.
Unlike COF/CdS and $\mathrm{COF/In}_{2}{\mathrm S}_{3}$ heterostructures, a different type of COF/MS heterostructure has been constructed by combining COFs with metal sulfides such as $\mathrm{Mo}_{3}\mathrm{S}_{13}$ , $\mathsf{M o S}_{2}$ , NiS, and $\mathbf{S}\mathbf{n}\mathbf{S}_{2}$ , which act merely as cocatalysts in the photocatalytic HER (Figure 6a). These metal sulfides merely act as cocatalysts in the photocatalytic HER. In 2018, the EB $\mathrm{COF}/\mathrm{Mo}_{3}\mathrm{S}_{13}$ heterostructure was reported as the first example by Ma & Zang et al (Figure 6b).[65] The anionic $\mathrm{Mo}_{3}\mathrm{S}_{13}$ was encapsulated in the pores of the cationic EB COF via electrostatic action, which converted the homogeneous $\mathrm{Mo}_{3}\mathrm{S}_{13}$ into heterogeneous photocatalyst. The $\mathrm{Mo}_{3}\mathrm{S}_{13}$ acts as a sink for photo-generated electrons and as an active site for HER. In addition, the stability and recyclability of the system were enhanced by the immobilization of the $\mathrm{Mo}_{3}\mathrm{S}_{13}$ . As a result, a remarkable HER activity of 13.215 mmol $\ensuremath{\mathrm{h^{-1}~}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{g^{-1}}}$ was obtained under irradiation $\lambda>420~\mathrm{nm}$ (Figure 6c). Subsequently, in the work of Dong, Xi & Zhang et al. in 2022, $\mathrm{Mo}_{3}\mathrm{S}_{13}$ was incorporated into the pores of TpPa-1 COF to construct a heterostructure.[66] In addition to promoting charge separation, the confinement effects of the pores in the COF prevent the aggregation as well as the dissolution of the $\mathsf{M o}_{3}\mathsf{S}_{13}$ cluster. Thus, 4.7 times higher photocatalytic HER activity compared with the pristine COF, was achieved by the optimal heterostructure. This work demonstrates that even non-ionic COFs can serve as porous supports for the homogeneous distribution and stabilization of the ionic metal sulfide clusters, thereby extending the range of COFs available for $\mathrm{COF}/\mathrm{Mo}_{3}\mathrm{S}_{13}$ heterostructure photocatalysts.
Similarly, as one of the most important noble metal-free cocatalysts for photocatalytic HER, monolayer $\mathrm{MoS}_{2}$ nanosheets have also been combined with COFs as heterostructure photocatalysts for HER. The first ${\mathrm{COF}}/{\mathrm{MoS}_{2}}$ heterostructure was reported in 2019 by Zhang et al (Figure 6d).[36] The TpPa-1-COF was synthesized by button-up from the molecular monomer on $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ nanosheets. The $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ nanosheets were prepared by a hydrothermal and sonicated strategy. The $\mathsf{M o S}_{2}$ nanosheets facilitated the separation of photo-generated charge carriers by attracting the electrons. As a result, the 3 wt. $\%$ $\mathsf{M o S}_{2}/\mathsf{C O F}$ heterostructure exhibits a 32-fold higher photocatalytic HER activity of $5.585\mathrm{mmol}$ $\ensuremath{\mathrm{h^{-1}~}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{g^{-1}}}$ under visible light $\left(>420\ \mathrm{nm}\right)$ irradiation compared to the pristine COF (Figure 6e). Moreover, the HER activity of this $\mathsf{M o S}_{2}/\mathsf{C O F}$ heterostructure photocatalyst even exceeds that of the corresponding Pt-based photocatalyst $(5.479\mathrm{mmol}\mathrm{h}^{-1}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ . This research highlights the effectiveness of $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ nanosheet cocatalysts in enhancing the photocatalytic activities of COF. In a recent work in 2022, an innovative nano- ${\bf\cdot M o S}_{2}@{\bf C O F\cdot P h}$ was prepared by in situ hydrothermal synthesis of ${\mathrm{MoS}}_{2}$ nanospheres from the Mo & S precursors with the as-synthesized COF.[67] The charge separation in a Z-Scheme pathway was further enhanced as a cooperation of the $\pi$ -conjugation system of the COF and the special configuration of the heterostructure. The heterostructure shows a photocatalytic HER activity of $7.2519\ \mathrm{mmol}\ \mathrm{h}^{-1}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ after content optimization, under irradiation of $\lambda>420\mathrm{nm}$ visible light.
In addition, $\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ and NiS are also effective noble metal-free HER cocatalysts in COF-based heterostructure photocatalysts. In 2021, the 2-D/2-D stannic sulfide $(\mathrm{SnS}_{2})/\mathrm{TpPa}..1$ COF heterostructure was assembled by hydrothermal deposition of $\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ nanosheets on the pre-synthesized 2D COF (Figure 6f).[38] The $\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ acts as both an electron collector and a catalytic site for the evolution of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ , resulting in a HER activity of 3.711 mmol $\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ when exposed to visible light. This activity is 21.7 times and 2 times higher for the pure COF and Pt/COF composites, respectively. Later, the NiS/TpBD COF heterostructure was prepared in a similar manner. The heterostructure shows HER activity up to $3.84\mathrm{mmol}\mathrm{h}^{-1}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ under visible light irradiation (Figure 6g).[68] All these results suggest that, as in many of the well-studied heterostructure photocatalysts, some metal sulfides are promising candidates to replace Pt in photocatalytic HER for COF. It is expected that more metal sulfides, especially the 2D nanosheets, could be explored as Pt-free photocatalytic HER cocatalysts for COF. The brief summary of the photocatalytic performances of the ${\mathrm{COF/MS}}_{\mathrm{x}}$ heterostructures in HER are listed in Table 2.
## 3.1.2. Photocatalytic HER Based on COF/MOs Heterostructure
Compared to metal sulfides, there are fewer reports on COF/metal oxide (MO) heterostructures for photocatalytic HER. This is possibly owing to the lower conduction band (C.B.) reduction potential and large band gaps of the MOs are not preferred in photocatalytic HER, although the metal oxides are more photostable and environmentally friendly (Figure 7a).
In view of the above shortcomings, COF can be used as a photosensitizer for the metal oxide to extend the light sensitive range. A typical case in this regard is the COF/anatase $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ heterostructure. Anatase $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ is the most studied metal oxide due to its high activity, stability and environmental friendliness. However, the performance of anatase $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ in photocatalytic HER under visible light irradiation is hampered by its $3.2\mathrm{eV}$ band gap. In the work of Zhang et al. in 2020,[45] the as-prepared anatase $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NPs with [001] facet exposed were first decorated with 3-aminopropyltriethoxysilane (APTES) (Figure 7b). The TpPa-1 COF was then crystallized on the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}.$ /APTES nanocrystals. The resulting optimized heterostructure shows an enhanced HER activity of 11.19 mmol $\ensuremath{\mathbf{h}}^{-1}\ \ensuremath{\mathbf{g}}^{-1}$ under visible light irradiation (Figure 7c). This enhanced activity is due to the injection of the excited electron in the LUMO (Lowest Unoccupied Molecular Orbit) of COF, excited by visible light irradiation, into the conduction band of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ . In addition, the covalent bond between COF and $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ was strengthened by APTES, facilitating the charge separation in a Type II manner, which further enhanced the HER activity. Following this work, the research on ${\mathrm{COF}}/{\mathrm{TiO}}_{2}$ heterostructures mainly focused on improving the surface textures of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ . It is discovered by Zhang et al. that the supercritical (SC) $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ facilitates the diffusion of Ti precursor into the as-synthesized TbBp COF shell and favors the formation of Ti–N bond. The prepared heterostructure consists of uniformly dispersed $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NPs, which are tightly bonded to the COF shell (Figure 7d). The extended photon response range combined with enhanced release of photo-generated electron–hole pairs was achieved. As a result, 25-fold and 4.5-fold enhanced photocatalytic HER activities up to 3.962 mmol $\mathbf{g}^{-1}\mathbf{h}^{-1}$ were recorded under visible light irradiation, respectively, compared to pure $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and conventionally prepared ${\mathrm{COF}}/{\mathrm{TiO}}_{2}$ heterostructures. Recently, a heterostructure composed of 2D COF and 2D $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ nanosheet has also been carried out (Figure 7e).[46] In the work of Shi et al, the TpPa-1 COF was prepared by a mechanism gridding method, and the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ nanosheet was prepared using HF as a surface directing agent. The as-prepared COF and $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ nanosheet were subjected to hydrothermal treatment to construct the heterostructure (Figure 7f).[69] The 2D morphology of each component promotes the maximum intimate contact as well as a minimum diffusion length for the photo-generated electron flow. As a result, HER activity of up to $1.37\mathrm{mmol}\mathrm{h}^{-1}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ was obtained under visible light irradiation (Figure $^{7\mathrm{g,h}}$ ).
In addition to the morphology control strategy, another way to modify $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ is the creation of oxygen vacancies. As pointed out by many previous works, the oxygen vacancies in $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ extend the absorption to the visible region. Meanwhile, the oxygen vacancies further affect the photocatalytic activities by influencing the separation/recombination of photo-generated carriers. Inspired by these findings, the TpPa-1 $\mathrm{COF/TiO_{2\cdotx}}$ heterostructure was recently discovered by Zhang’s group (Figure 8a–c).[70] Black $\mathrm{TiO}_{2\mathrm{-}\mathrm{x}}$ was prepared by treating the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NPs with $\mathrm{NaBH}_{4}$ , and serves as a support for the fabrication of TpPa-1 COF from the molecular building blocks. The Z-Scheme separation processes for electron–hole pairs were presented (Figure 8a), resulting in the enhanced visible light HER rate of $15.33\mathrm{mmolh^{-1}g^{-1}}$ for the optimized sample (Figure 8c). Another strategy in the design of $\mathrm{COF}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ heterostructures for photocatalytic HER is the metalinsulator-semiconductor (MIS) system. In the work of Xu & Long et al. in 2022, the MIS system was constructed by first synthesizing the TP COF in situ on the as-synthesized anatase $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ with {001} facet exposed, and second incorporating the PVP-capped Pt NPs in situ in the photocatalytic HER processes. The accelerated charge separation was observed as a result of the electron transport in the C.B. of the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ . Thus, an enhanced visible light induced HER activity of $25.9\mathrm{mmol}\mathrm{h}^{-1}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ was achieved compared to the pristine TP-COF together with the $\mathrm{TP/TiO}_{2}$ heterostructure, using $\mathrm{Pt}$ as cocatalyst for all.[71]
In addition to $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ , the heterostructure composed of COF and other metal oxides has also been studied. In the work of Zhang et al, a Z-Scheme heterostructure was prepared by buttonup synthesis of TpPa-2 COF on $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ (Figure 8d).[72] The optimized heterostructure show a visible light induced photocatalytic HER activity of 3.77 mmol $\ensuremath{\mathrm{h^{-1}~}}\ensuremath{\mathbf{g}}^{-1}$ in the absence of Pt cocatalyst (Figure 8e), as a result of enhanced integration between the COF and the $\scriptstyle\alpha\cdot\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ . Recently, the ${\mathrm{COF}}/{\mathrm{WO}}_{3}$ heterostructures have emerged because the ${\tt W O}_{3}$ photocatalyst has a narrower band gap. In these heterostructures, Z-Scheme charge separation processes are necessary because the C.B. reduction potential of $\mathrm{\WO}_{3}$ is lower than that for HER. In the work of Wei, Wang & Zhang et al, the $\mathrm{WO}_{3}@\mathrm{TpPa}.1.\mathrm{COF}/\mathrm{rGO}$ heterostructures were constructed.[73] The $\ensuremath{\mathbb{W}}\ensuremath{\operatorname{O}_{3}}$ was etched during the in situ growth of COF on its surface. The photo-deposition method was applied to load the electron collecting rGO via reducing its oxidative precursor GO. The ternary heterostructure shows a HER activity of $26.73\mathrm{mmol}\mathrm{h}^{-1}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ induced by visible light irradiation, due to the narrowed band gap and the facilitated charge separation. A similar work on ${\mathrm{COF}}/{\mathrm{WO}}_{3}$ heterostructures was carried out soon by Fang, $\mathtt{X u}\&$ Ma et al. In their work, the rGO was not included in the heterostructure, and direct Z-Scheme heterostructures were constructed between TpPa-1-COF and $\ensuremath{\mathbb{W}}\ensuremath{\operatorname{O}_{3}}$ (Figure 8f).[74] A visible light-induced photocatalytic HER activity of $19.89\mathrm{mmol}\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ was observed (Figure $\mathbf{8g})$ ), ascribing to the strong interaction between the COF and the ${\tt W O}_{3}$ . These works not only enrich the type metal oxide supports in COF-based heterostructure HER photocatalysts, but also extend the light responsive range to the visible region. The brief summary of the photocatalytic performances of the COF/metal oxide heterostructures in HER are listed in Table 3.
## 3.1.3. Photocatalytic HER Based on COF/Carbon Materials Heterostructure
Carbon materials include graphene derivatives, carbon nitrides and metal carbides. Like metal sulfides, the roles of carbon materials in photocatalytic HER include both photocatalyst and cocatalyst. Inspired by the remarkable performance of carbon materials in photocatalytic HER, the heterostructures composed of COF and carbon materials are expected to show further enhanced photocatalytic HER activities.
The work on COF/Car. heterostructure was first carried out in 2020.[75] In the work of Zhang et al. rGO (reduced graphene oxide) was used as a host for the in situ synthesis of TpPA-1 COF. rGO was chosen because of its high electron conductivity and large specific surface area due to its special 2D configuration (Figure 9a). In the preparation processes, GO and the monomers of COF were subjected to a one-pot solvothermal treatment. This can simultaneously lead to the in situ assembly of TpPA-1 COF on the surface of GO via covalent bonding and the in situ reductive formation of rGO. Under visible light irradiation, the resulting optimized $\mathrm{COF/rGO}$ heterostructure exhibits HER activity of up to $\mathrm{11.98\mmolh^{-1}g^{-1}}$ after Pt loading. These results confirm the role of rGO as an electron transfer mediator in promoting the separation of electron–hole pairs generated by irradiation. Subsequently, the Pt/TpPA-1 COF/rGO ternary heterostructure with Pt selectively anchored on rGO was reported by the same group through a one-step photoreduction of $\mathrm{Pt^{4+}}$ and GO on the asprepared COF TpPA-1. The selective photo-deposition of Pt NPs was achieved as a result of the anchoring functional group on the surface of GO (Figure 9b).[76] Compared to previous works, this configuration further facilitates the release of irradiationgenerated carriers, resulting in a further enhanced photocatalytic HER activity of 19.59 mmol $\ensuremath{\mathrm{h^{-1}~}}\ensuremath{\mathbf{g}}^{-1}$ under irradiation with $\lambda>$ $420\mathrm{nm}$ (Figure 9c).
Recently, MXenes, a group of 2D transition metal carbides/carbonitrides/nitrides, have emerged as another type of carbon-based cocatalyst in photocatalytic HER, as they exhibit strong coupling with the substrate, high electron conductivity and charge separation properties. In 2020, a pioneering work on COF/MXene heterostructure HER photocatalyst was carried out by Zhao et al. by in situ growing various COFs on the surface of pre-treated $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -functionalised $\mathrm{NH}_{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{\mathrm{x}}$ (Figure 9d).[77] The NTU-BDA-THAT COF/MXene heterostructure shows the best photocatalytic activity of $14.23\mathrm{mmol}\mathrm{h}^{-1}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ stably under visible light irradiation with wavelength greater than $420\mathrm{nm}$ (Figure 9e). All these works demonstrate the effectiveness of carbon materials in promoting charge separation as cocatalysts in photocatalytic HER.
Another important carbon material in photocatalytic HER is carbon nitride $(\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{N}_{4})$ . Since its discovery in 2009 by Wang et al, $\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ has attracted increasing attention. This is because $\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ not only has a suitable energy band structure $(\mathrm{E_{g}}=2.78\:\mathrm{eV})$ for HER using visible light irradiation, but also carbon nitride is composed of non-metallic light elements that are abundant on earth. Moreover, like graphene, the 2D configuration of $\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ facilitates the migration and separation of photo-generated carriers. All these advantages contribute to the remarkable catalytic activity of $\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ under visible light irradiation.
In 2020, Yan et al. combined the TTA-TP COF with the $\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ nanosheets (CNSs) to construct the first COF/CN heterostructure. The CNSs were prepared by sonicating the bulk precursor and small amounts of TTA-TP COF were assembled in situ on the surface via imine condensation.[54] The COF inactivate the termination at the surface of the CNS boundary region and strengthen the electronic coupling between them. These two contributions cooperatively enhance the charge separation processes. As a result, an enhanced photocatalytic HER yield of 46.4 mmol $\ensuremath{\mathrm{h}}^{-1}$ $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ under visible light irradiation was achieved after optimizing the content. Later, by tuning the morphology of the COF, the 1D TpBD COF/2D $\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ heterostructures were successfully constructed by Tan, Ho and Li et al (Figure 9f).[43] In this work, by taking advantage of the reversibility of the imine bonds, the 1D morphology of the COF was realized by in situ crystallization transformation of the amorphous 1D nanofibers on the surface of the 2D $\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ . The charge transport and separation efficiency can be enhanced due to the large and good interfacial contact. As a result, the heterostructure with optimized mass ratio shows a photocatalytic $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ evolution yield of $11.3~\mathrm{mmol~h^{-1}~g^{-1}}$ under irradiation with $\lambda>420~\mathrm{nm}$ (Figure $|9\mathrm{g}\rangle$ . Furthermore, it is interesting to note that this heterostructure is particularly effective under irradiation with longer wavelengths $(500\mathrm{nm})$ .
As discussed above in the section on $\mathrm{COF/MS\&COF/MO}$ heterostructures, the 2D/2D stacking configurations outperform the 1D/2D ones, as the 2D/2D stacking allows the maximum interfacial contact to facilitate the charge transfer and separation. Similar work was also carried out by Zhang et al in 2022, the 2D/2D $\mathrm{IpPa-1-COF/C_{3}N_{4}}$ (g) heterostructure was constructed, by heat treatment to both as-synthesized COF and $\mathsf{g}{\mathrm{-}}\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{N}_{4}$ in aqueous solution.[55] The charge separation was conducted intermolecularly, resulting in a visible light photocatalytic HER activity of 1.15 mmol $\ensuremath{\mathrm{h^{-1}~}}\ensuremath{\mathbf{g}}^{-1}$ . In addition. In addition, one of the shortcomings of the $\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ photocatalyst is its dependence on the noble metal $\mathrm{Pt}$ as a co-catalyst in HER. While in the work of Jiang et al in 2021, it is interesting to note that under visible light irradiation, the TP-DPBA $\mathrm{COF}/\mathrm{g}–\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ heterostructure shows a photocatalytic HER activity of 11.73 mmol $\ensuremath{\mathrm{h^{-1}~}}\ensuremath{\mathbf{g}}^{-1}$ . This activity becomes comparable to those Pt-containing photocatalytic systems, and overwhelms other Pt-free systems. The possible reasons can be described as the effective adsorption of irradiation with wavelength greater than $420\mathrm{nm}$ together with enhanced carrier separation. Meanwhile, the HER activity can be further enhanced by adding Pt cocatalyst. This work adds new knowledge on the design and preparation of noble metal-free photocatalytic HER system.[56] Furthermore, in the work of Wang et al, the one-pot strategy was used to construct the ternary TMP COF from three kinds of building monomers: TAPT (2,4,6-tris(4-aminophenyl)- 1,3,5-triazine), Melem, & TP (1,3,5-triformylphloroglucinol), in situ on the surface of the $\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ .[57] This strategy allows the design and preparation of the COFs with higher intermolecular charge transfer efficiencies as well as higher absorption to simulated solar irradiation. This is due to the increased difference in electron densities between the building monomers. Unlike the prevailing heterostructures with staggered energy band positions for the constituents, in this TMP ternary $\mathrm{COF}/\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ heterostructure, the charge separations follow a Type I fashion. Consequently, the photocatalytic HER activity for the TMP $\mathrm{COF}/\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ heterostructure was remarkably enhanced to $102.88\upmu{\mathrm{mol}}\mathrm{h}^{-1}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ under Xe lamp irradiation compared to the pristine $\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ and TM (binary) $\mathrm{COF}/\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ heterostructures. The brief summary of the photocatalytic performances of the COF/carbon material heterostructures in HER are listed in Table 4.
## 3.1.4. Photocatalytic HER Based on COF/MOF Heterostructure
As another kind of crystalline and porous material, Metal– Organic Frameworks (MOF) shows attractive properties. MOFs are assembled by the coordination of organic molecular and metal clusters have been used in various fields as a crystalline and porous materials. To date, there are many MOF-based photocatalysts for a range of reactions. It is reasonable to expect that COF/MOF heterostructures could be more efficient for photocatalytic HER.
The first report on COF/MOF heterostructure HER photocatalyst was made by Lan et al. in 2018 (Figure 10a–c).[37] The TpPa-1 COF was synthesized in situ from the molecular building blocks on the surface of the pre-synthesized $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO-66 via imine condensation (Figure 10b). Due to the high specific area of the surface, effective absorption to visible light irradiation and effective charge separation within the heterostructure, 20 times higher photocatalytic HER activity, up to $23.41\mathrm{mmol}\mathrm{h}^{-1}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ was achieved under irradiation with $\lambda>420~\mathrm{nm}$ (Figure 10c). Similar work was carried out in 2019 by Zou et al. to construct heterostructures consisting of CTF $\mathrm{COF}/\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-125 $(\mathrm{Zr})$ or (Ti) metal–organic framework. The photocatalytic HER performance of the heterostructure is 0.36 mmol $\mathbf{g}^{-1}\mathbf{\Lambda}\mathbf{h}^{-1}$ using visible light irradiation (Figure 11a–c).[41]
In order to improve the order of each component in the heterostructure, epitaxial growth is used to construct the core/shell COF/MOF heterostructure. In the work of Jiang et al. in 2020 (Figure 11a,d),[59] the $\mathsf{M O F@C O F}$ core/shell heterostructure is prepared via a two-step in situ growth of the stable hydrazonelinked TFPT-DETH COF on $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO-66 MOF surface. Compared with a random physical mixture, the epitaxial heterostructure exhibits a significantly enhanced photocatalytic activity of $7.178~\mathrm{mmol~h^{-1}~g^{-1}}$ under irradiation with wavelength greater than $420\mathrm{nm}$ , which can be explained by more effective charge recombination restraint and photon absorption by the heterostructure (Figure 11e). This work demonstrates that the activities of a heterostructure in photocatalysis can be further enhanced by configuration optimization. A brief summary of the photocatalytic performances of the COF/carbon material heterostructures in HER are listed in Table 5.
One challenge in the preparation of covalently bonded COF/MOF heterostructures is that the $\boldsymbol{-}\mathrm{N}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ in the ligand of the MOF is a required functional group for bonding to the COF via a Schiff base reaction. A possible solution has been proposed by Zhang & Yan et al. in 2020 (Figure 10d).[39] In their work, the $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ cluster of an as-prepared $\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ -free MOF-808 was decorated with an $\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ group by reaction with $p$ -aminobenzoic acid. The core/shell MOF-808 $@$ TpPa-1-COF heterostructures were successfully prepared by in situ growth of COF on MOF-808 with modified surface. The resulting MOF-808@COF (TpPa-1) heterostructure with an optimal mass fraction shows photocatalytic HER activity of $11.88\mathrm{mmol}\mathrm{h}^{-1}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , which was achieved under visible light irradiation, 5.6 times compared with the pure COF. It is expected that the synthetic strategy of this work can be extended to other MOFs, leading to the COF/MOF heterostructure with richer structural diversity.
In addition, MOFs are expected to be a noble metal-free cocatalyst for photocatalytic HER, as MOFs are rich in different metal single atomic sites. A typical case of this point is the work of Tang et al. in 2022, as shown in Figure 10e.[60] The heterostructure was prepared by mechanically mixing of the as-prepared Tp-Pa-2 COF and the $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTP})_{2}$ MOF. Similar to the Pt cocatalysts, the MOF promotes charge separation due to its conductivity. Under $\lambda>420~\mathrm{nm}$ (visible light) irradiation, the mass-majorized COF (Tp-Pa-2)/MOF $\mathrm{(Cu_{3}(H H T P)_{2})}$ ) heterostructure shows a HER activity of 7.71 mmol $\ensuremath{\mathrm{h^{-1}~}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{g^{-1}}}$ , which is 297 times higher than that of the pure COF (Figure 10f).
## 3.1.5. Photocatalytic HER on Other COF-Based Heterostructure
In addition to the works discussed above, a newly emerged COF-based heterostructure for photocatalytic HER is the COF/transition metal phosphides (TMPS). Recently, the TpPa-1 COF was assembled in situ on the surface of three types of asprepared TMPS in the work of Ma et al.[78] The TpPa-1/ $/\mathrm{Ni}_{12}\mathrm{P}_{5}$ shows the most outstanding visible light catalyzed HER capability of 3.16 mmol $\ensuremath{\mathrm{h^{-1}~}}\ensuremath{\mathbf{g}}^{-1}$ . The roles of these TMPS can be described as electron collectors and active sites for lowering the energy of intermediate formation during the reaction. Given the rich chemical compositions and structures for the TMPS, it is expected that more COF/TMPS heterostructures will be carried out in the future. Furthermore, the atomic precision of the TMPS may facilitate the understanding of the possible photocatalytic process at the molecular level.
Based on the above results and discussions, it can be inferred that photocatalytic HER is the first and most studied application for all kinds of COF-based heterostructures. Considering the crucial role of HER in addressing the energy shortage and the advantages of COF-based heterostructure, a bright future can be reasonably expected for COF-based heterostructure HER photocatalysts. The principles for designing effective COF-based heterostructure HER photocatalysts can be summarized as follows: 1) The reduction potential of the resulting C.B. should be more negative than $-0.41\mathrm{~V~}$ to trigger the reduction of proton, while the oxidation potential for the resulting V.B. (Valance Band) should be positioned in a balanced consideration between the oxidation of sacrificial agent and the absorption of solar irradiation; 2) For the Z-Scheme COF-based heterostructure, the band gap of the support should be considered for effective utilization of visible light irradiation; 3) For one of the most prevailing COF-based heterostructures COF/CdS, the stability of the CdS is very important in consideration of environmental friendliness and practical application. Specific solutions can be classified as 1) Avoiding the accumulation of photo-generated holes in the CdS by managing the charge separation paths (Type II or Z-Scheme) according to the energy band positions of the COF and CdS, 2) Taking advantage of the shielding effective of the COF by in situ assembling the CdS NPs beneath the cores of the as-synthesized COF, preventing their contact with the reactants, 3) Choosing suitable hole sacrificial -agents. The designing principle for CdS stabilization should be applied to all kinds of COF/CdS heterostructures, regardless of the reaction types.
## 3.2. Carbon Dioxide Reduction Reaction (CDRR)
Photocatalytic reduction of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ has attracted increasing attention as a green process that reduces the amount of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ in the Earth’s atmosphere. Meanwhile, some value-added compounds such as CO, $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and HCOOH can be produced in the CDRR. Compared with other photocatalysts, the COF-based heterostructures are promising candidates for CDRR due to their designable structure, high light absorption and enhanced charge separation.
## 3.2.1. Photocatalytic CDRR Based on COF/Carbon Material Heterostructure
The first work on photocatalytic CDRR using COF-based heterostructure photocatalysts was carried out by Wang et al. in 2020.[42] As shown in Figure 12a–c, in their work, the carbon quantum dots were first prepared from the pyrolysis of metalloporphyrin (M-PCD) and immobilized in the pores of assynthesized TD COF via sonication and thermal treatment under $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ (Figure 12b). This configuration not only facilitates the adsorption of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ , but also prevents the leaching of the homogeneous carbon quantum dots. As a result, the M-PCD $@$ TDCOF heterostructure exhibits a CO evolution of 0.5 mmol $\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ along with $98\%$ CO to $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ selectivity under visible light irradiation, using $[\mathrm{Ru(bpy)}_{3}]^{2+}$ (bipyridine–ruthenium complexes) to further enhance the light absorption (Figure 12c). Later, Do et al. fabricated a $\mathrm{COF/rGO}$ photocatalyst via in situ synthesis of TpPa-1 COF on graphene oxide accompanied by in situ reduction of GO to rGO (Figure 12d). The rGO promotes the effective utilization of photo-generated carriers, along with increasing the absorption of visible light irradiation.[79] In this work, an optimized CO evolution rate of $0.2~\mathrm{mmol~h^{-1}~g^{-1}}$ and $89\%$ CO to $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ selectivity under visible light irradiation were achieved in a stable manner (Figure 12e). Another carbon-based material that combines with COF to form the heterostructure for photocatalytic CDRR is defective $\mathrm{g}{\cdot}\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ . In the work of Ye et al. in 2022,[80] it is discovered that the N-vacancies in the $\mathsf{g}{\mathrm{-}}\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{N}_{4}$ increase the Fermi level difference between the TP-TTA COF and $\mathrm{g}{\cdot}\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ . Consequently, an accelerated S-Scheme charge transfer is obtained (Figure 13a,b), which promotes the CO production amount to $0.576\mathrm{mmol}\mathrm{h}^{-1}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , together with $90.4\%$ CO to $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ selectivity under visible light irradiation (Figure 13c). More recently, a series of novel COF-organic molecular heterostructures were studied by Zhou et al. as an effective photocatalysts for CDRR. In their work, the Anthracene, Pyrene, and Perylene were assembled on the as-prepared TMBen COF via an end-capping strategy. The formation of covalent bond facilitates the charge separation through a Type II pathway, minimizing the charge recombination. Amongst, the TMBen-Perylene heterostructure shows the highest CDRR activity up to $93.0\upmu\mathrm{mol}\mathrm{h}^{-1}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , under visible light irradiation, using TEOA as the hole sacrificial agent.[81]
## 3.2.2. Photocatalytic CDRR Based on COF/Metal Sulfide Heterostructure
In addition, the COF/metal sulfide has recently been explored as a photocatalyst for CDRR. In the work of Wang et al, the COF/CdS heterostructure was prepared by in situ imine condensation of the molecular building blocks $Z\mathrm{n}$ -TAPP & Co-Bpy on the surface of the as-prepared CdS nanowires (Figure 12f).[82] They discovered that the CdS nanowires with 1D morphology facilitate the intramolecular charge transfer between the donor ( $Z\mathrm{n}$ -TAPP) and the acceptor (Co-Bpy) within the frameworks. Thus, under visible light irradiation, the heterostructure shows a carbon monoxide evolution rate of $0.507\mathrm{mmol}\mathrm{h}^{-1}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and a CO selectivity of $72\%$ (Figure 12g).
## 3.2.3. Photocatalytic CDRR Based on COF/Metal Oxide Heterostructures
Compared with carbon material and metal sulfides, metal oxides are more environmentally friendly and take the advantage of reactant adsorption. As one of the most effective photocatalysts, anatase $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ is also combined with COF for CDRR. In the work of Liu et al, the Tp-Tta COF was constructed in situ from the molecular monomers on as-prepared $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ nanocrystals with {001} & $\{101\}$ facets exposed, as shown in Figure 14a,b.[83] they discovered that the Tp-Tta ${\mathrm{COF}}/{\mathrm{TiO}}_{2}$ {101} heterostructure exhibits better photocatalytic activity due to the higher electron density on the surface and the lower conduction band. Under simulated solar irradiation, the CO generation activity of $580~\mathrm{\textmumol}$ $\ensuremath{\mathrm{h^{-1}~}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{g^{-1}}}$ could be achieved by the optimized S-Scheme Tp-Tta $\mathrm{COF}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ {101} heterostructure (Figure 14c). This activity is at the peak with the conducted COF-based heterostructure applied in photocatalytic CDRR. This work highlights the importance of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption in photocatalytic CDRR, which is further confirmed by the recent work of Liu et al. Whilst this work focuses on $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ splitting, it will be discussed in a later section. The brief summary of the photocatalytic performances of the COF-based heterostructures in CDRR are listed in Table 6.
From the above discussion, it can be inferred that the COFbased heterostructures in photocatalytic CDRR are still an area to be explored in the future. The principles for designing effective COF-based heterostructure CDRR photocatalysts are similar to those for HER photocatalysts with the following differences: 1) The reduction potential of the resulting C.B. should be more negative $(-0.53\mathrm{\:V}$ vs NHE for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CO})$ ; 2) The active sites, such as metal-porphyrin, metal-bipyridine, and electron-rich oxygen can be introduced into the COFs as well as the supports to facilitate the adsorption and activation of the linear inert $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecule.
## 3.3. Oxygen Evolution Reaction (OER)
OER is another important process in photocatalytic reactions, including $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ splitting, $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ splitting, and $\Nu_{2}$ reduction reaction.[84,85] The meanings of OER are not only the evolution of $\mathrm{~O}_{2}$ , but more important the consumption of the photo-generated holes, facilitating the overall splitting reactions. OER can be one of the rate-determining process in these reactions. This is because first, OER is a four-electron transfer process, and second, the generation of $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ is kinetically sluggish, as a result of the high overpotential.
So far, there are some reports on electrocatalytic OER based on COF and COF-based heterostructures containing electroactive functional groups.[86–89] However, photocatalytic OER via COF and COF-based heterostructures is still in its infancy. This is possibly due to the insufficient oxidation capability of the photogenerated holes in the HOMO (or V.B.) of the COF and the instability of the organic framework in the oxidative environment. Fortunately, based on the working principle and the application of COF-based heterostructure in other related photocatalytic reactions, it can be expected that the Z-Scheme heterostructure based on COF can be a candidate for photocatalytic OER, where the electrons in the COF contribute to the reduction of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ or electron sacrificial agents and the holes in the support (e.g., $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ) contribute to the water oxidation to evolve $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ .[17,47] Moreover, the additional potential advantages of COF in photocatalytic OER can be summarized as: 1) the integration of electroactive molecular structures,[88] and 2) the introduction of highly active monodisperse single atomic active sites.[89]
## 3.4. Water Splitting Reaction
Compared to photocatalytic HER, photocatalytic water splitting is considered more promising for sustainable $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ production. This is because it can take place in pure water without the use of holesacrificial agents. However, it remains a challenging reaction due to the much lower product yields. The bottleneck for efficient water splitting is found to be the kinetically sluggish four-electron oxygen evolution reaction (OER).
As it is discussed in the HER and OER sections above, COFbased heterostructures are promising candidates for water splitting. To take into account the V.B. oxidation potential and stability for the water oxidation half reaction, the supports are metal oxides (Figure 15a). The first work is carried out by Li and Chen et al. in 2022.[90] In their work, the dual cocatalysts $\scriptstyle\alpha\cdot\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ & FeP-PC were first prepared by heating the same precursor MIL-88B(Fe) in oxidative and inert atmospheres, respectively (Figure 15b). Both were then modified with APTES. The TpPa-1 COF was then prepared by imine condensation of the molecular building blocks on these as-prepared cocatalysts. The agglomeration of the COF as well as the recombination between electrons and holes induced by visible light irradiation were inhibited due to the high porosity of the iron support. The charge separation was further facilitated by the Z-Scheme heterostructure between the COF (TpPa-1) and $\alpha$ -ferric oxide. The iron phosphide (FeP)-PC acts as an HER cocatalyst and the OER takes place on the $\boldsymbol{\alpha}{\cdot}\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ . All this results in $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ evolution rates of 97.45 and $48.68\upmu\mathrm{mol}\mathrm{h}^{-1}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , respectively, under irradiation with $\lambda>420\mathrm{nm}$ (Figure 15c). Later, a prominent work was carried out by Lan et al. that exploited the synergistic effect of piezo catalysis and photocatalysis.[91] In this work, the TpPa-1 COF was synthesized in situ on the surface of the as-prepared APTES-BiFeO3 (BFO), forming a core/shell configuration Z-Scheme heterostructure (Figure 15d). As a result of the cooperative effect of the polarized electric field and visible light irradiation, the separation of photo-generated carriers was remarkably promoted. Therefore, the optimized COF (TpPa1)/BFO heterostructure shows the $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ evolution rates of 1416.4 and $708.2\ \mathrm{\textmumol\h^{-1}\ g^{-1}}$ , respectively, in the presence of both simulated solar irradiation and ultrasound (Figure 15e–g). The result is the highest among the COF-based heterostructure photocatalysts. Based on the insight into the reaction intermediates, Z-Scheme pathways for electron–hole separation are proposed. The significance of this work is that the energy from external fields can be used to promote the sluggish photocatalytic water splitting system in addition to irradiation. The brief summary of the photocatalytic performances of the COF-based heterostructures in water splitting are listed in Table 7.
Based on the above discussion, it can be concluded that photocatalytic water splitting via COF-based heterostructure is still at an early stage compared to HER. It is expected that future work in this field will focus on the more effectively using of visible light illumination, combined with the cooperation of energy from external fields. Moreover, the types of supports are expected to be extended to metal sulfides, & MOFs et al. The design principle for COF-based heterostructure for photocatalytic water splitting can be summarized as following (Figure 9): 1) The reduction potential of the resulting C.B. should be more negative than $-0.41\mathrm{\V}$ while the oxidation potential of the resulting V.B. should be more positive than $0.82\mathrm{V}$ to trigger the reduction of proton and oxidation of water. 2) The overpotentials should be considered in the design of the C.B. and V.B. 3) External field sensitive supports can be combined with COF to further promote the photocatalytic activities.
## 3.5. $\mathsf{c o}_{2}$ Splitting Reaction
Compared to CDRR with hole-sacrificing agents such as TEOA, photocatalytic conversion of carbon dioxide to fuels using $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ as a sacrificial agent for the photo-generated holes, called $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ splitting (also called artificial photosynthesis), is undoubtedly more useful and economical for practical applications. Like $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ splitting, the $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ splitting is more challenging than CDRR due to the rate-determining four-electron Oxygen Evolution Reaction (OER). Carbon materials and metal sulfides are rarely used as supports in account of their V.B. oxidation potential (Figure 10a). The highest CO generation rate for $_{1\mathrm{g}}$ of photocatalyst in 1 hour is currently 0.0858 mmol.
## 3.5.1. Photocatalytic $C O_{2}$ Splitting Based on COF/Metal Oxide Heterostructures
The first work on photocatalytic $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ splitting via COF-based heterostructure is carried out by Lan et al. in 2020.[17] As shown in Figure 10b, the dioxin-linked COF-318 & COF-316 were synthesized in situ and covalently linked on the surface of the asprepared metal oxides, such as anatase $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ with exposed [001] facets, $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ , & ${\tt B i}_{2}\tt{W O}_{6}$ . Among them, the COF (318)/anatase $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ [001] shows the most outstanding $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ splitting performances up to $69.67\upmu\mathrm{mol}\mathrm{h}^{-1}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ upon irradiation $\langle\lambda>=380\mathrm{nm}_{.}$ ). Experimental investigations and theoretical calculations suggest a direct Z-Scheme electron–hole separation process within COF & $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ (Figure 10c). Thus, the separation of photo-generated electron–hole pairs was promoted. The $\mathrm{e^{-}}$ in the C.B. of COF contribute to the reduction of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ to CO, the $\mathrm{{h^{+}}}$ in the V.B. of anatase $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ contribute to the oxidation of water. All these lead to the enhanced photocatalytic $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ splitting performance.
Inspired by this pioneering work, a rapid growth of the COFbased heterostructure for photocatalytic $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ splitting has been observed. Wang et al. prepared the $\mathrm{CuP-Ph/TiO}_{2}$ heterostructures in 2021 via the in situ growth of the COF from the building blocks [5,10,15,20-tetrakis(4-aminophenyl) porphinato divalent copper (CuTAPP) and $p$ -benzaldehyde (Figure 16d).[92] The tight binding between the COF and $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ was realized through the iso-nicotinic acid group and the Z-Scheme heterostructure was constructed. The heterostructure shows a remarkably enhanced CO production of $50.5\ \upmu\mathrm{mol}\ \mathrm{h}^{-1}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , with improved stability under Xe lamp irradiation without filter (Figure 16e). Later, the work of Lan et al. successfully prepared three-component COF-based heterostructures.[47] First, COF-366 was synthesized from its building blocks DHA and Ni-TAPP on the as-prepared
MOF $(\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-125 (Ti)). Second, the COF/MOF composites were transformed into the yolk-shell three-component $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL125/TiO2@COF-366-Ni-OH-HAc (MTCN-H ys) heterostructure by acetic acid etching with controlled concentration, temperature, and time. The coexistence of Z-Scheme and Type II heterostructure with the COF, MOF and $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ effectively promote the charge separation. In addition, the heterostructure extends the light sensitive area and increases the exposed active sites. The heterostructure shows an enhanced CO yield up to $17\upmu\mathrm{mol}\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ under simulated solar light. All these works highlight the role of COF-based Z-Scheme heterostructure in promoting photocatalytic $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ splitting activities.
As described above, UV irradiation is essential for the ZScheme heterostructure composed of COF and anatase $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ due to the large $\mathrm{E_{g}}$ of anatase $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ . To take advantage of visible light irradiation, the Type II heterostructures are also constructed using COF as a photosensitizer (Figure 17a). In the recent work of Bi et al, the $318\mathrm{\COF}$ was grown on the 1D titanium dioxide nanofiber (TNF) (Figure 17b). Energy band structures for each component contribute to a Type II heterostructure under visible irradiation.[44] In addition, the characteristic 1D morphologies of the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ TNF further accelerate diffusion and prevent separation for the photo-generated carriers. The CDRR takes place in the C.B. of the TNF, while the OER takes place in the V.B. of the COF. The heterostructure with optimized mass ratio shows a 12.7 times higher CO yield of ${70.1\pm0.7\upmu\mathrm{mol}\mathrm{h}^{-1}\mathrm{g}^{-1}}$ under irradiation of $\lambda>420\mathrm{nm}$ , with a stoichiometric CO and $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ generation ratio of 2:1 (Figure 17c). This work shed light on carbon dioxide splitting under visible light irradiation. The key points enabling the effective $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ splitting in the Type II heterostructure can be described as the reductive potential of C.B. in the support oxide should be higher than that for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CO}$ $(-0.53\mathrm{V}$ vs NHE). Meanwhile, the oxidative potential of the V.B. in the COF should be lower than that for $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}/\mathrm{O}_{2}$ . Later, the same group carried out a new work by growing 318-COF in situ on the wide bandgap metal oxide ${\beta{\cdot}{\bf{G}}{\bf{a}}_{2}{\bf{O}}_{3}}$ .[93] Likewise, the mass optimized heterostructure shows a CO yield of $85.8\ \upmu\mathrm{mol\h^{-1}\ g^{-1}}$ under irradiation with $\lambda$ $>420\mathrm{nm}$ , resulting from the enhanced charge separation of the wide band gap ${\beta{\cdot}{\bf{G}}{\bf{a}}_{2}{\bf{O}}_{3}}$ and proper energy band structures. The above work verifies that visible light irradiation, the main component of solar irradiation, can be adopted for photocatalytic $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ splitting.
As discussed in the CDRR section above, the interactions between the photocatalyst and the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ are important for the subsequent photocatalytic reduction. This has also been demonstrated for photocatalytic $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ splitting. In the work of Liu et al. COF-5 was synthesized in situ from the molecular monomers HTTP (2,3,6,7,10,11-hexahydroxytriphenylene) and PBBA (1,4- phenylenediboronic acid) on the surface of hydrothermally asprepared CoAl-LDH (Co & Al Layered Double Hydroxides) (Figure 17d).[94] The CoAl-LDH is rich in metal atoms coordinated by six-fold hydroxyl groups, and surfaces with such a structure favor the adsorption of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and the stabilization of the intermediate $\mathrm{COOH^{\ast}}$ . The energy band structure of COF and LDH contributes to the staggered Type II heterostructure under visible light irradiation, inhibiting the coupling of carriers generated by photon absorption of COF. As a result of the cooperative effect between the promoted substrate adsorption and the enhanced charge separation, a CO generation selectivity of $96.4\%$ together with a CO generation rate of $0.0663~\mathrm{mmol~h^{-1}~g^{-1}}$ has been achieved in the optimal COF-5/CoAL-LDH heterostructure (Figure 17e). The activity is 4.8 and 2.3 times higher than that of COF-5 and LDH, respectively.
More recently, inspired by their previous work on the TMP $\mathrm{COF}/\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ heterostructure for photocatalytic HER, Wang et al. prepared the TMP ${\mathrm{COF}}/{\mathrm{TiO}}_{2}$ heterostructure for photocatalytic $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ splitting.[95] The results show that the atomic-level unit modulation strategy is also effective in facilitating charge separation when the heterostructure is constructed with $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ , as evidenced by the enhanced CO generation ratio of $6.76\upmu\mathrm{mol}\mathrm{h}^{-1}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ under $\lambda>420\mathrm{nm}$ irradiation.
## 3.5.2. Photocatalytic $C O_{2}$ Splitting Based on COF/MOF Heterostructures
One of the shortcomings of the well-studied COF/metal oxide heterostructure $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ splitting photocatalysts is the insufficient $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption on the surface of the metal oxides. Therefore, it is expected that the MOFs with eminent surface texture, such as high specific surface area and well-distributed pores, are promising candidates as supports in the COF-based heterostructure. In
2022, Huang & Bi et al. prepared the COF/MOF heterostructure for photocatalytic $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ splitting for the first time (Figure 16f).[96] In this work, a novel olefin-linked COF, TT, was first synthesized via a base-catalyzed Knovengal reaction from its molecular building blocks TMT (2,4,6-trimethyl-1,3,5-triazine) and TFPT (1,3,5- tris(4-formylphenyl)-triazine). The $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO-66 $(\mathrm{Zr})$ MOF was synthesized in situ with the COF by solvothermal treatment of the corresponding inorganic salt and the organic ligand, which promoted the formation of the heterostructure. An S-Scheme heterostructure prevents the recombination of photo-generated charges. Furthermore, the enhanced $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption was verified by analyzing the in situ FTIR results. As a result, the $15\%$ TT COF/ $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO-66 $(\mathrm{Zr})$ MOF shows advanced carbon monoxide generation up to $6.56~{\upmu\mathrm{mol}}~\mathrm{h}^{-1}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and trace amount of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ under visible light irradiation (Figure $16\mathrm{g,h}$ ). Not long after, Wang et al. in situ synthesized the TP-TA from the molecular building blocks on the surface of three kinds of as prepared $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL MOFs with the same ligand but different metal centers (Figure 17f).[97] It is found that all three types of heterostructures exhibit enhanced photocatalytic activities compared to pristine COF and MOF under simulated solar irradiation. More importantly, the order of activity within the COF/MOF heterostructure is as follows: COF/MOF $(\mathrm{NH_{2}{\cdot}M I L{\cdot}G8(I n)})>\mathrm{COF/MOF}~($ $(\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ - $\mathrm{MIL-66(Zr))>COF/MOF}$ $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-101(Fe)) (Figure $17\mathrm{g})$ . They attribute this order to the reductive potential of the MOF. Meanwhile, the Type II charge separation processes have been verified by this order, as the MOFs act as sinks for photo-generated electrons.
From these works, it can be concluded that COF/MOF heterostructures are promising candidates as photocatalysts for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ splitting, although related research is still in its infancy. Compared to metal oxide, MOFs have the advantage of more effective $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption due to their framework structure. In addition, the structural diversity of MOFs provides opportunities for the preparation of new COF/MOF heterostructure $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ -splitting photocatalysts in the future.
## 3.5.3. Photocatalytic $C O_{2}$ Splitting Based on COF/Clusters
A new type of COF-based heterostructure $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ -splitting photocatalyst is the COF/cluster. The cluster here refers to the polyoxometalate (POM). The advantages of POM clusters include, but are not limited to, tunable electronic and geometric structure, and atomic scale composition. Furthermore, the disadvantages such as poor stability, low surface area and easy aggregation can be overcome by confinement in the pores of the COF. In the work of Lan et al, the aminated $\mathrm{{MnMo}_{6}}$ POM cluster was confined into the pores of aldehyde group-containing COFs via imine covalent bonding.[98] Under visible light irradiation, enhanced charge separation was observed, with the POM clusters acting as electron collectors for the reduction of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and the COFs preserving holes for the oxidation of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ . In addition, the POM clusters, as catalytically active sites, lower the activation energies of the inert $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ . All this contributes to the CO formation rate of $37.25\upmu\mathrm{mol}$ $\ensuremath{\mathrm{h^{-1}~}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{g^{-1}}}$ with $100\%$ selectivity, the best results for COF and POM based catalysts. The brief summary of the photocatalytic performances of the COF-based heterostructures in water splitting are listed in Table 8.
On the Basis of the above discussions, it can be concluded that more and more studies will be paid on COF-based heterostructures for photocatalytic $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ splitting in the future. It is expected that future work in this field will focus on the more effective use of visible light illumination, adsorptive activation of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ , together with the cooperation of energy from external fields. The design principle for COF-based heterostructure for photocatalytic $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ splitting can be summarized as follows: 1) The resulting C.B. should have higher reduction potential than that for CDRR, while the oxidation potential of the resulting V.B. is similar to that of water splitting, 2) The overpotentials should also be considered in the design of the heterostructure, 3) The activation of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ should be considered in the design of the COF and selecting support.
## 4. Summary and Outlook
The crucial role of photocatalytic fuel generation reactions has resulted in a surge in the development of efficient photocatalysts. In this review, we summarize the up-to-date progress of the COF-based heterostructure photocatalysts in fuel generation reactions, including the $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ evolution reaction, $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction reaction, $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ evolution reaction, $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ splitting, and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ splitting. We discussed the preparation method, characteristics, and the applicable reactions for different types of heterostructure. In particular, we attempt to clarify the reasons for the enhanced activities of the COF-based heterostructure photocatalysts, namely, facilitating the charge separation, expanding the light responsive range, and providing the reactive sites. Moreover, the designing principles for COF-based heterostructure photocatalysts targeting at different types of reactions were proposed.
The ultimate goal of research on photocatalysis is the design and scalable production of higher efficiency photocatalysts. With this in mind, and enlightened by these recent achievements on COF-based heterostructure photocatalysts, it can be expected that in the future, research on the following topics will be promising to achieve the above ultimate goal.
1) In the design of the COFs in the COF-based heterostructure, attention is merely paid to taking advantage of the donor– acceptor (D–A) type structure to facilitate the intramolecular charge transfer. While in the research on pristine COF with excellent photocatalytic HER activity, the introduction of hyper-hydrophilic molecular monomers into the framework has been conducted. It is therefore expected that this strategy can be used to enhance the activity of the COF-based heterostructure photocatalysts by facilitating the adsorption of the reaction substrates. For the CDRR and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ splitting reaction, the metal porphyrins are effective for the adsorption and activation of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ . Thus, metal porphyrins can be used as molecular building units in the synthesis of COFs in COF-based heterostructure photocatalysts. Another possible advantage of this strategy is that it may facilitate the formation of more intimate interfaces between the COF and the supports, further promoting interfacial charge separation.
2) The modification of the supports in the heterostructure is also crucial. The advantages of this strategy are similar to those for COFs. Some works based on this strategy has been carried out. More methods, such as doping, morphology control, pore generation and vacant engineering, are expected to be carried out in the future.
3) By analyzing the dynamic process of HER and CDRR, it is found that the sacrificial agent (SA) adsorption is as important as the substrate adsorption in determining the overall photocatalytic activity. However, SA adsorption is almost ignored in the design and preparation of COF-based heterostructure photocatalysts. Therefore, it is reasonable to expect that the COF-based heterostructure with enhanced SA adsorption will be designed and prepared in the future.
4) New methods are expected to be introduced to rational design of the COF and the support. These new methodologies include but not limited to artificial intelligence machine learning and theoretical calculations.
5) From the point of view of practical applications, the recycling of photocatalysts should be considered, and the introduction of external fields is prevalent. Therefore, the preparation of COF-based heterostructure photocatalytic films and the fabrication of devices using the COF-based heterostructure as the key component are reasonable. In addition, the advantages of COF-based heterostructure in films and devices are that the layer-by-layer assembly is facilitated as a result of the 2D characteristic of most COFs.
6) Some other important photocatalytic reactions related to the renewable energies, such as the selective oxidation of methane, the selective two-electron Oxygen Reduction Reaction to produce $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ , and Nitrogen Reduction Reaction to generate $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ could be explored.
